What the hunks do, by file (each is exact-context matched against the pinned version at build time, so a puppeteer upgrade
that moves any of them fails the build instead of silently shipping a half-patched copy):

  cdp/FrameManager.js   Runtime.enable is not sent. A frame's main world is found with a context-less Runtime.evaluate of
                        globalThis (idOnly), its utility world from Page.createIsolatedWorld, both on first use after each
                        navigation; wait tasks pending at a navigation are re-run.
  cdp/IsolatedWorld.js  the lazy context provider those two use.
  cdp/WebWorker.js      a worker's context is found the same way; Runtime.enable is not sent to it.
  cdp/Frame.js          exposeFunction bindings are installed in the main world.
  cdp/ExecutionContext  the script name puppeteer appends to everything it evaluates (pptr:evaluate;<caller's file>) is
                        not sent.
  api/Frame.js, api/ElementHandle.js, common/QueryHandler.js
                        evaluate / evaluateHandle / $eval / $$eval / waitForFunction / waitForSelector run in the
                        utility world, unless the code opts into the page's own world with a leading "//!world=main".
  node/ChromeLauncher.js  NOT patched, on purpose. OMP's hunk drops puppeteer's default --disable-features list; a throwaway keeps it. The
                        detection page flags nothing with the list or without it, and without it WebUIOmniboxPopup and WebUIOmniboxAimPopup
                        are no longer disabled: Chrome then starts one more renderer process per browser (measured: 12 renderers for
                        4 browsers against 8, ~180 MB private at idle).

